<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="sec-study-skills">
  <title>Math Study Skills</title>

  <introduction>
    <p><url href="https://www.learningscientists.org/">The Learning Scientists</url> are a group of educators and researchers who aim to provide research-based best practices for studying and learning. Their focus is on six major study strategies that can be used for any subject.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsubsection xml:id="sec-study-skills-when-you-study">
    <title>When You Study</title>

    <p>The first two strategies are used to determine when you study material.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Spaced Practice</title>

      <p>Think about a young baseball player who is learning to hit fastballs. During batting practice each day, the pitcher is going to throw a bunch of fastballs so that the player can work on his timing, the point of contact, and recognizing what a fastball looks like coming out of the pitcher’s hand until he has it down and is good at it.</p>

      <p>This is spaced practice, or spreading out the work at regular intervals, which gives your brain time to develop the neuron pathways to deal with ideas and concepts.</p>

      <p>This is <url href="https://youtu.be/3WJYp98eys8?si=fviIecnH-DCf1EeH">spaced practice</url>, or spreading out the work at regular intervals, which gives your brain time to develop the neuron pathways to deal with the ideas and concepts.</p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Interleaving</title>

      <p>If batting practice stops here, then the player has not learned how to hit a fastball when it counts. While he might get really good at hitting fastballs, an opposing pitcher in a game is not likely to throw him another one once he realizes that the player has done the work to hit a fastball. Instead, the practice pitcher also needs to spend some time throwing a variety of pitches so that the player can practice seeing fastballs out of context, learn to spot the differences between how a fastball comes at him as opposed to how a curveball or change up looks, and adjust his swing accordingly.</p>

      <p>This is <url href="https://youtu.be/kV64Bu6sec0?si=OiNkwx-SJZcKLpmp">interleaving</url>, or switching between topics and ideas to help you learn to distinguish between concepts and when to apply different strategies.</p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Incorporating Spaced Practice and Interleaving</title>

      <p>It is almost impossible to “accidentally” use spaced practice or interleaving. To ensure that you are using these strategies, you must make a plan to do SOMETHING everyday. Even five minutes a day can be effective if it is done consistently. Use one of these resources to develop a plan that will help you space your studying out and use interleaving.</p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><url href="https://algonquin-academic-success.libguides.com/study-skills/time-management">Create a weekly schedule and a semester plan</url> (use your syllabi for this!)</li>
          <li><url href="https://asc.tamu.edu/getmedia/f16c73ba-4f56-4961-9e35-dd6047ec30a0/5-Day-Study-Plan_1.pdf">Five-Day Study Plan for Exams</url></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection xml:id="sec-study-skills-how-you-study">
    <title>How You Study</title>

    <p>The remaining strategies are techniques used to do the actual studying and learning.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Retrieval Practice</title>

      <p>Think about going to dinner at a new friend’s home. Imagine they ask you to set the table while they step out to take a phone call. You are in a pickle because, since you have never been there before, you don’t know where anything is. You just have to start opening cabinets and drawers until you find plates, cups, dinnerware, and salt and pepper. Eventually, you will find everything you need and get it on the table.</p>

      <p>Now imagine that dinner went well, and you and this person are starting to become good friends. They invite you over for dinner again the next week and once again ask you to set the table. This time you may still have to open some unnecessary cabinets, but fewer than last time and you are able to set the table much faster.</p>

      <p>When you have gotten out the stuff you need to set the table over and over again, you eventually get to the point where you know where the stuff is and don’t have to think about which cabinet to get a cup from or where the dinner knives are. You can go directly to the spot where those items are stored immediately and without effort.</p>

      <p>This is <url href="https://youtu.be/Pjrqc6UMDKM?si=BLF7E-2C5tir_QNg">retrieval practice</url>, or recalling previously learned concepts and procedures without using notes or resources, and is actually <em>the most important strategy</em> and you should do this <em>every single time</em> you study. But it is <em>hard</em> and can lead to being under-confident in your abilities compared to just rereading your notes or simply working old homework problems with your notes in front of you. If you have ever experienced “going blank” on a test after you felt confident and well-prepared, a lack of properly done retrieval practice might be the cause.</p>

      <p>Here are some ways you can incorporate retrieval practice:</p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>Flash cards - most people do not use flashcards in a way that is actually beneficial. If you feel like they are a waste of time, take a look at these three resources for using flashcards and give them a try. You may find that the way you use them matters more than you thought.</li>
          <li><url href="https://www.learningscientists.org/blog/2016/2/20-1?rq=flashcards">How to Study with Flashcards</url></li>
          <li>If you get stuck with the instruction cards, use ChatGPT or another generative AI program to help generate some.</li>
          <li><url href="https://youtu.be/l4PD_Vg0SnI?si=5dyijRsobjB5k4em">How To Use Flashcards Effectively</url></li>
          <li><url href="https://www.learningscientists.org/blog/2019/4/4-1?rq=flashcards">The 5-Sided Flashcard</url></li>
          <li><url href="https://midlandedu-my.sharepoint.com/:w:/g/personal/jkneisley_midland_edu/EcmMInRPZs1NtnTYqrUz0hABB5aINS7NHCXE9quQ9bmNtQ?e=M3TTqP">Take a Timeout</url></li>
          <li><url href="https://universitycollege.asu.edu/microlearning-videos/self-quizzing">Quizzing/Self-Testing</url></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Other Techniques</title>

      <p>These techniques are effective strategies on their own but can be used as part of your retrieval practice.</p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><url href="https://youtu.be/gQRzW24KrDc?si=rysQOflQb3u6ArIP">Elaboration</url></li>
          <li><url href="https://youtu.be/7wF0lCBMh9Y?si=WBO4qkzgG7uwf8iZ">Concrete Examples</url></li>
          <li><url href="https://youtu.be/6xCZ4XnkpCc?si=AyLRCz1hzSGalqwD">Dual Coding</url></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Resources for Studying for Math</title>

      <p>What resources you use to prepare to communicate your understanding is up to you. Those resources could include but are not limited to the following. Please keep in mind that, as described above, studying effectively and efficiently for math includes far more than doing the assigned homework problems.</p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>eBook textbook</li>
          <li>Physical copy of the book</li>
          <li>Problems from the book</li>
          <li>Additional text books</li>
          <li>Free worksheets with answer keys found online</li>
          <li>Reviews (in Canvas)</li>
          <li>Lecture Prep for each section (worksheet available in Canvas for use in conjunction with the book)</li>
          <li>Effective study strategies as described above</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection xml:id="sec-study-skills-underutilized-resource">
    <title>An Often Overlooked and Underutilized Resource</title>

    <p>Students taking online courses often say they feel like an island, alone in an ocean of unknown content with no one else around. Research shows that those who feel connected to their classmates do better in online classes than those who do not.</p>

    <p>One way to combat this isolation is to create an effective study group, even if it is via Teams or Zoom, and get together regularly to work on course materials. To find a group, post some general times when you are available in the Ask a Peer|Help a Peer discussion or respond to someone who has already posted.</p>
  </subsubsection>
</subsection>